nodecode-windows-services is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 1d1027f864aa3c1388eac08e6eee84fb862fbe91
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes plugin in Python: a plugin.yaml declaring two tools, `win_service_table`
and `win_service_detail`, one PowerShell `Get-CimInstance Win32_Service` call whose JSON the
plugin parsed, a `winreg` half that read the dependency edges, the delayed-start flag and the
`FailureActions` blob out of HKLM, a second PowerShell call for the Service Control Manager's
event log, and two tables of words -- Win32 exit codes and SCM event ids -- kept in
exitcodes.py. This rewrite keeps the same two tools by the same names, reaching the same two
sources and reporting the same facts: the plugin ran the registry half through Python's winreg,
the cell reads those values in the same PowerShell process as the listing (so one child process
answers for the whole machine instead of one per service), and decodes the 32-bit
`SERVICE_FAILURE_ACTIONS` blob in Lisp out of the byte array PowerShell hands over, with the
exit-code and event-id tables carried over word for word. The filters, the 400-row ceiling and
the distinction between a failed exit code and 1077 (never tried since the last boot) are the
plugin's, as is the note that the output reveals what is installed and how it runs. What was
left out is what belonged to the runtime rather than to the tools: plugin.yaml, the pyproject
packaging, the pip dependency list (it was empty) and the pytest suites that need a live
Windows box, replaced by the cell's section declaration, its own `.asd`, and an offline suite
that stubs the one child process. Nothing of the original is vendored.
